 "name": "HDR / Exposure / Bloom Lab",
 "_note": "Every surface here is UNLIT (pbr_shade.wgsl's object.flags.z branch), there is no fog and no sky, so a patch's scene-linear radiance is exactly baseColor + emissiveColor * emissiveIntensity -- the number written in this file and nothing else. That is the whole point of the fixture: this lab owns what the chain DOES with a radiance, and a fixture whose radiance came out of a BRDF would make every reading a joint measurement of the Lighting Lab's code and this one's. The camera sits 20 m back with a 40 degree vertical field of view, so the visible plane at z = 0 is 25.8824 x 14.5589 m and the 4 x 4 wedge fills it one patch per sixteenth of the frame: a case samples the centre of cell (col,row) at ((col+0.5)/4, (row+0.5)/4) in normalised screen coordinates and needs no projection of its own. Row 0 is the wedge up to scene white, row 1 above it to the extreme this engine can actually author -- 50, which is the hard maximum of the material/emissive parameter (procedural.cpp), and a scene file that writes 256 is silently given 50, row 2 is one channel at a time at radiance 2 with the neutral control beside them, row 3 is the two hues Glowmere is made of plus a second control pair at 8. The bright sources for the bloom arms are 100 m to the right on the same black ground, as a second page the case's camera selects: 0.3 m across (14.8 px of a 720-line frame, smaller than one texel of bloom level 4) and 4.0 m across (198 px, thirty of them). Nothing in this file authors post settings -- a case sets those through the post/* parameters, which is what the application writes.",
